doc:aliensAppealsBoard a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "the Aliens Appeals Board"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityJudicial ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "A request for a preliminary ruling was submitted to the CJEU in a judgment delivered by the general assembly of the Aliens Appeals Board on 8 December 2016, in a case bearing some similarities to the present case ( X and X v. Belgium , C-638/16 PPU)."^^xsd:string,
        "An action to have a decision by the Aliens Office set aside does not suspend it. For this reason, section 39/82 of the Aliens Act provides for the possibility of submitting a request for a stay of execution either under an “ordinary” procedure or under the “extremely urgent” procedure. In principle, requests for suspension and for setting aside must be submitted in the same application, except in cases of extreme urgency. In the latter case, the request for a stay of execution may be submitted separately from the action to have the decision set aside. However, the latter must be submitted within 30 days of notification of the decision to refuse the visa, failing which the stay of execution and any other interim measures ordered prior to submission of the action to have the decision set aside will be lifted. A stay of execution may be ordered only if the grounds relied on are sufficiently serious to warrant setting aside the impugned decision, and if immediate enforcement of the decision is liable to cause serious damage that it would be difficult to repair."^^xsd:string,
        "If the Aliens Office refuses to issue a visa, the requesting party may apply to the Aliens Appeals Board to have the Aliens Office’s decision set aside. The Aliens Appeals Board is an administrative court which rules in a collegial formation or as a single judge."^^xsd:string,
        "the Aliens Appeals Board"^^xsd:string .

doc:brusselsTpiDutch a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "Brussels Dutch‐speaking Court of First Instance (TPI)"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityJudicial ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "lodged a unilateral action to that effect with the President of the Brussels Dutch ‐ speaking TPI on 13 December 2016, but was unsuccessful (order of the same date)."^^xsd:string .

doc:domestic_proceeding_aliens_appeals_board_stay_2016_10_20 a echr:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "Aliens Appeals Board judgment ordering stay of execution (20 October 2016)"@en ;
    echr:followsProceeding doc:administrative_action_aliens_office_refusal_2016_10_17 ;
    echr:hasCourt doc:aliensAppealsBoard ;
    echr:hasDecisionDate "2016-10-20"^^xsd:date ;
    echr:hasInstanceLevel echr:LevelAdministrativeReview ;
    echr:hasOutcome echr:OutcomeMeasureImposed ;
    echr:hasOutcomeDirection echr:DirectionFavourableToApplicant ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:participation_applicants_aliens_appeals_2016_10_20,
        doc:participation_belgian_state_aliens_appeals_2016_10_20 ;
    echr:hasProceedingType echr:TypeAdministrative ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "By a judgment of 20 October 2016 the Aliens Appeals Board, essentially reiterating the substance of its previous judgments, ordered a stay of execution of the refusal decisions of 17 October 2016. In addition, in view of the imminent danger faced by the applicants, the Belgian State’s persistent refusal to comply with res judicata authority and the importance of ensuring that the remedy was effective, it considered that it was justified to direct the State to issue the applicants, within 48 hours, with laissez-passers or visas, valid for three months."^^xsd:string .

doc:enforcementJudgeBrussels a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "enforcement judge"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityJudicial ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "In the event of difficulties in executing a judgment containing an order to pay penalties, any interested party can apply to the enforcement judge (“ juge des saisies ”) on the basis of Article 1498 of the Judicial Code, without, however, such an application having suspensive effect."^^xsd:string,
        "ordered the applicants not to pursue further enforcement proceedings pending a decision by the enforcement judge who was to rule on the validity of the enforcement procedure and the State’s right to restrict the amounts due in respect of penalty payments."^^xsd:string .

doc:aliensOffice a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "the Aliens Office"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityAdministrative ;
    echr:hasAuthorityName "the Aliens Office"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "In a judgment of 7 March 2017, the CJEU reiterated that under Article 1 of the Visa Code, the Code concerned visas for stays not exceeding 90 days. In the case in question, however, the visa applications had been lodged for the purpose of claiming asylum and therefore of being issued with a residence permit with a validity that was not restricted to 90 days. The CJEU thus concluded that those applications, although formally submitted on the basis of Article 25 of the Visa Code, fell outside the scope of the Code, in particular of Article 25 (1) (a). Furthermore, as no measure had been adopted by the EU legislature with regard to the conditions governing the issue by member States of long-term visas and residence permits on humanitarian grounds, the applications at issue fell solely within the scope of national law."^^xsd:string,
        "The majority of so-called “humanitarian” visas were issued to family members who did not meet the legal requirements for family reunion. Since the CJEU’s judgment of 7 March 2017, only long-stay visas are issued to persons applying for visas who wish to claim asylum in Belgium."^^xsd:string,
        "Until the CJEU’s judgment of 7 March 2017 (see paragraphs 71-73 below) short-stay visas could be issued on humanitarian grounds to individuals in disturbing medical or humanitarian circumstances; to aliens entitled, at the invitation of the Belgian authorities, to submit an asylum claim in Belgium; to beneficiaries of resettlement programmes run by the Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees and, in Belgium, by the Commissioner-General’s Office for Refugees and Stateless Persons; and to asylum-seekers who had been involved in an exceptional rescue operation, on the instruction of the Secretary of State for Immigration and Asylum Policy."^^xsd:string,
        "the Aliens Office, the administrative body responsible for issuing visas"^^xsd:string .

doc:brusselsCourtOfAppeal a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "Brussels Court of Appeal"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityJudicial ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "On an appeal by the applicants, the Brussels Court of Appeal delivered a judgment on 7 December 2016 varying the order of 7 November 2016."^^xsd:string .

doc:brusselsTpi a echr:DomesticAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "Brussels French-language Court of First Instance (TPI)"@en ;
    echr:hasAuthorityKind echr:AuthorityJudicial ;
    echr:hasJurisdictionState "Belgium"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote """Article 584 of the Judicial Code provides for the possibility of applying to the president of the court of first instance by means of an urgent application or by an ex parte application:
“The President of the Court of First Instance shall, in respect of all matters except those which the law excludes from the jurisdiction of the courts of justice, give a provisional ruling in cases which he recognises as being urgent."""^^xsd:string,
        "The matter shall be brought before the President by means of an urgent application or, in cases of absolute necessity, an ex parte application."^^xsd:string,
        "the applicants brought a unilateral application before the President of the Brussels French-language Court of First Instance (“TPI”)."^^xsd:string .

doc:domestic_proceeding_aliens_appeals_board_set_aside_2017_03_24 a echr:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "Aliens Appeals Board judgment dismissing set-aside application (24 March 2017)"@en ;
    echr:followsProceeding doc:domestic_proceeding_aliens_appeals_board_stay_2016_10_20 ;
    echr:hasCourt doc:aliensAppealsBoard ;
    echr:hasDecisionDate "2017-03-24"^^xsd:date ;
    echr:hasInstanceLevel echr:LevelAdministrativeReview ;
    echr:hasOutcome echr:OutcomeClaimDismissed ;
    echr:hasOutcomeDirection echr:DirectionUnfavourableToApplicant ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:participation_applicants_aliens_appeals_2017_03_24 ;
    echr:hasProceedingType echr:TypeAdministrative ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "On 24 March 2017 the Aliens Appeals Board dismissed the application to have those decisions set aside, finding that the Aliens Office’s decisions of 13 September 2016 (see paragraph 11 above) had become final and that, in consequence, the applicants no longer had standing to challenge the subsequent decisions."^^xsd:string .

doc:partyBelgianState a echr:Party ;
    rdfs:label "the Belgian State"@en ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "For its part, the Belgian State opposed, before the same courts, the granting of visas."^^xsd:string .

doc:partyTheApplicants a echr:NaturalPerson,
        echr:Party ;
    rdfs:label "the applicants"@en ;
    echr:hasPersonName "a married couple and their two minor children"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "The case originated in an application (no. 3599/18) against Belgium lodged with the Court under Article 34 of the Convention on 10 January 2018 by a married couple and their two minor children, all Syrian nationals (\"the applicants\")."^^xsd:string ;
    echr:isRepresentedBy doc:cassationLawyer .

